m":"New Britain Water Department"}],"more":0}],"first_year":2004,"last_year":2026,"explain":{"Lead":{"plain":"Lead is almost never in water at its source. It gets picked up on the way to the tap \u2014 from the service line under the street, from solder in older joints, from the brass in the tap itself. That makes it a property of your building more than of your town.","effect":"Infants and children who drink water containing lead in excess of the action level could experience delays in their physical or mental development. Children could show slight deficits in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)":{"plain":"Like trihalomethanes, haloacetic acids form when disinfectant reacts with organic matter in the water. They are a side-effect of disinfection, not an added ingredient.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing haloacetic acids in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Haloacetic Acids","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)"},"Combined Uranium":{"plain":"Uranium occurs naturally in some bedrock and dissolves into groundwater. It is a chemical concern for the kidneys as well as a radiological one.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing uranium in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer and kidney toxicity.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Combined Uranium"},"TTHM":{"plain":"Trihalomethanes are not added to water. They form when the chlorine used to disinfect it reacts with natural organic matter already present. They are a by-product of making water safe from bacteria.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing trihalomethanes in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could experience problems with their liver, kidneys, or central nervous system, and may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Total Trihalomethanes","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"TTHM"},"Combined Radium (-226 and -228)":{"plain":"Radium is naturally radioactive and dissolves out of certain rock formations into groundwater. Like arsenic, it is a feature of local geology.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing radium 226 or 228 in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Combined Radium (-226 and -228)"},"Gross Alpha, Excl. Radon and U":{"plain":"Gross alpha is a screening measurement rather than a single substance. It counts alpha-particle radioactivity from naturally occurring elements in rock, and a high reading prompts testing for the specific ones.","effect":"Certain minerals are radioactive and may emit a form of radiation known as alpha radiation. Some people who drink water containing alpha emitters in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Radionuclides","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Gross Alpha, Excl. Radon and U"},"Nitrate":{"plain":"Nitrate usually comes from fertiliser run-off, septic systems, or manure. It is most common in farming areas and in shallow wells.","effect":"Infants below the age of six months who drink water containing nitrate in excess of the maximum contaminant level could become seriously ill and, if untreated, may die. Symptoms include shortness of breath and blue-baby syndrome.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Nitrate","who":"Infants under six months. This is the fastest-acting risk on this list.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Nitrate"},"Lead and Copper Rule":{"kind":"rule","name":"Lead and Copper Rule","plain":"The rule governing lead and copper monitoring and corrosion control. A violation is often procedural \u2014 a missed sampling round or a late report.","effect":null,"source":null,"who":null}}}